  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given,</p><p>In a group of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn></math>&nbsp;persons&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>75</mn></math>&nbsp;speak English and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn></math>&nbsp;speak Hindi,&nbsp;</p><p>And each person speaks at least one of the two languages,</p><p>And the number of persons who speak only English is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>&nbsp;and the number of persons who speaks only Hindi is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi></math>,</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0046_4d894c194c7f.png\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 197px;\" /></p><p>Now from above diagram we can see that,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>75</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></math></p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>75</mn><mo>-</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></math></p><p>Hence, number of people who speak English only is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></math>&nbsp;and Hindi is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></math>,</p><p>Now putting the value in given equation of ellipse we get,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>25</mn><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>60</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mn>25</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>25</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p>Now comparing with&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;we get,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p>Now we know that,&nbsp;</p><p>Eccentricity of ellipse is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>1</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>25</mn><mrow><mn>36</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>119</mn></msqrt><mn>12</mn></mfrac></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
